From cd2388e583ca15a354d32a3794b64232b070d72d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: shaurizard <contact@nyoom.engineering>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 12:07:56 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 07/32] thread: block the timer queue's dispatch thread until
 its next timer

The timer queue's dispatch thread waited for new timers with
disruptorplus's spin_wait_strategy, which spins, then yields the time
slice 19 times out of 20 and sleeps 1 ms the 20th. With only the 1 ms
KeTimeStampBundle timer queued (HighResolutionTimer, xboxkrnl_module.cpp)
the thread never sleeps for long, and under Wine each yield is a system
call; on a Steam Deck that is CPU time taken from the APU's shared 15 W
budget.

Wait on a condition variable instead: until the earliest timer is due,
or without a timeout when the queue is empty, and wake when a timer is
queued. disruptorplus has a blocking_wait_strategy, but its timed waits
pass the predicate before the timeout to std::condition_variable and do
not compile, so the strategy is defined here. The empty-queue case no
longer passes time_point::max() as a deadline, which a condition
variable converts to the system clock and can overflow.

Condition variable timeouts on Windows are rounded up to the system
timer period (15.6 ms by default), so the queue holds a 1 ms period
(timeBeginPeriod) while it runs. Xenia Canary sets the process timer
resolution at startup for the same reason (src/xenia/base/main_win.cc,
NtSetTimerResolution). The destructor joins the dispatch thread before
releasing the period.

Part of the runtime changes that took Project Gotham Racing 4 on the
Steam Deck from 78.7 to 89.4 fps (pgr4-native README, "Steam Deck
(OLED)"); no run measures it on its own.

Co-authored-by: factory-droid[bot] <138933559+factory-droid[bot]@users.noreply.github.com>
